# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
from chapters17_a import CH, add
add(40, "A robin may navigate by seeing the Earth’s magnetic field, and plants may move energy through their leaves by quantum coherence. Both claims are live research and neither is settled, which makes the subject a model of how to tell a promising idea from a proven one.",
 "How do we know that quantum effects matter in living things? The rates of some enzyme reactions show isotope effects that only tunneling explains.",
 "What result would show that a bird’s compass depends on a quantum process and not on iron-bearing cells?",
 "Thermal energy at body temperature, kT, is about 0.027 electron-volts. Why does that make long-lived quantum coherence in a warm, wet cell so hard?")
add(41, "The human genome is about three billion letters. Printed in the type of this book it would fill about four thousand copies. The surprise of reading it was how few genes it holds, about twenty thousand, and how much else it contains.",
 "How do we know that a variant causes a disease and does not merely travel with it? We need statistics across many people, a mechanism, and, ideally, a test of the variant in cells.",
 "What would you conclude about a gene if people lacking it entirely were healthy?",
 "Two people differ at roughly four or five million places in their genomes, yet are 99.9 percent identical. Check the arithmetic against three billion letters.")
add(42, "In 2012 a bacterial immune system became a tool that can be aimed at any chosen stretch of DNA. Eleven years later the first CRISPR medicine, for sickle-cell disease, was approved.",
 "How do we know that an edit hit only the intended place? Genome-wide sequencing of edited cells looks for unintended cuts, and each method has a detection limit.",
 "What kind of unexpected result in a clinical trial would make you stop a therapy at once?",
 "Name one change you would accept in a patient’s own blood cells and one you would not accept in an embryo. What exactly separates them?")
add(43, "In 2010 a team built a bacterial genome from chemicals in a machine and used it to run a living cell. The DNA carried watermarks, including a quotation about creation and understanding. Writing life, and not only reading it, had begun.",
 "How do we know that a synthetic genome works? The cell boots, grows and divides, and its proteins match the designed sequence.",
 "What result would show that we do not yet understand a minimal cell’s genes?",
 "Think of an organism you would not want engineered. What safeguards would you require before anyone tried?")
add(44, "If Earth’s history were a single day, bacteria would appear before dawn, animals with shells at about nine in the evening, and the dinosaurs would vanish at about 11:40 at night. Humans would arrive in the last six seconds.",
 "How do we know the dates? Radioactive decay in volcanic ash layers gives ages, and independent methods check each other.",
 "A mass extinction found to have happened at very different times in different places would challenge a single cause. What would that suggest?",
 "Find a road cut or cliff showing layered rock. Which layers are oldest, and what evidence in the rock tells you?")
add(45, "Microbes live in boiling springs, in rock two kilometers underground, in acid as strong as battery fluid, and in ice. Each find stretches the definition of where life can exist, and so changes how we look for it on other worlds.",
 "How do we know that these organisms are growing and not merely surviving? Cultures increase in number, and isotopes show that they are metabolizing.",
 "A living cell found at 150 °C would change the temperature limit. What evidence would you need to be sure?",
 "List three conditions life needs everywhere it has been found. Name a place on another world that offers them.")
add(46, "Planets in the habitable zones of their stars are common, and so far every one of them is silent. Whether the silence means that life is rare or that it is hard to see is the question the next generation of telescopes is built to answer.",
 "How do we know that a gas is a biosignature and not geochemistry? We look at it in context, with several gases together, and ask whether any lifeless process could supply them.",
 "What would a confirmed, repeatable sign of life on another world do to our estimate of how common life is?",
 "Choose a value for each factor of the Drake equation and compute the result. Then change each factor within a plausible range, and see how the answer ranges from nobody to millions.")
add(47, "At least twenty species of human relatives have lived and died in the past seven million years, and for much of that time several lived at once. We are the last survivors of a crowded family, and our genomes still carry traces of two of the others.",
 "How do we know that our ancestors interbred with Neanderthals? Ancient DNA, which earned Svante Pääbo the 2022 Nobel Prize, shows their genes in living people outside Africa.",
 "A skeleton of a modern human found in clearly older rock than the oldest Homo sapiens fossils would change the story. What dating would you want?",
 "Lay out a strip of paper seven meters long, one millimeter for each thousand years. Where do the Neanderthals, the pyramids and you fall?")
add(48, "Your heart beats about three billion times in a lifetime and your body replaces most of its cells every few years, yet the temperature of your blood stays within a degree or so. That stability is not rest. It is constant correction.",
 "How do we know how the body works? William Harvey measured how much blood the heart pumps and showed that it must circulate, and every physiology textbook since has rested on that kind of measurement.",
 "A treatment that lowered blood pressure but increased deaths would show that the measurement was not the same as the outcome. Why can that happen?",
 "Count your pulse at rest, then after climbing stairs, and time how long it takes to return to normal. What does the delay tell you?")
add(49, "Smallpox killed hundreds of millions of people in the twentieth century alone and was declared eradicated in 1980. It shows what understanding germs, immunity and public health can do, and the later pages show what happens when the lessons are forgotten.",
 "How do we know that a microbe causes a disease? Koch’s tests ask whether it is present in cases, can be isolated, causes the disease in a new host and can be recovered again, and genomic evidence adds more.",
 "What would it take to show that a new pathogen can spread between people and does not merely cross from animals?",
 "Wash your hands the way a hospital does, with soap for twenty seconds, and notice how often you normally do not.")
add(50, "You carry about as many bacterial cells as human ones, some forty trillion of each, and they act as an organ you did not know you had. The best-proven medical use of them is, on first hearing, unpleasant: donated stool that cures a lethal infection.",
 "How do we know that microbes cause a condition and do not merely follow it? Germ-free mice that receive a microbiome can acquire traits, and transplants in people can cure certain infections.",
 "What kind of study would persuade you that a gut community causes depression, and not the reverse?",
 "Think of what you ate last week. Which habits could change your gut community fastest, and which slowest?")
add(51, "Two people with the same diagnosis can respond to the same drug in opposite ways, and sometimes one letter of DNA explains why. Medicine that reads that letter before prescribing is already routine for a few drugs and for some cancers.",
 "How do we know that a treatment works for an individual and not just on average? Trials, biomarkers and carefully designed single-patient studies each help.",
 "Why would a test that raises the risk of a very rare disease by a fifth change little, while the same rise in a common disease changes a lot?",
 "Take a disease with a 1 percent chance and a gene that raises it by 20 percent, and another with a 20 percent chance and the same rise. Work out the absolute changes.")
add(52, "Mice live two years or so. Naked mole-rats, no larger, live thirty and rarely get cancer, and a bowhead whale can live two centuries. Lifespan is not a fixed law of chemistry but a trait that evolution tunes, which is why it can in principle be changed.",
 "How do we know that an intervention extends lifespan in people? So far we do not. Trials need decades, so researchers look for markers that predict health.",
 "What result would show that a pill that works in mice also works in humans?",
 "Choose one product that claims to slow aging. Write down what evidence in humans would convince you.")
add(53, "The brain weighs about 1.4 kilograms, uses a fifth of the body’s energy and holds some eighty-six billion neurons, each connected to thousands of others. It does all this on roughly twenty watts, less than a bright light bulb.",
 "How do we know which region does what? Injuries, imaging, electrical stimulation and, in animals, turning chosen neurons on and off with light have all been used, and they have to agree.",
 "A brain region lit up in a scan is not necessarily needed for the task. What kind of experiment can show that it is?",
 "Close your left eye, look at a cross on a page and move a dot toward it from the right at arm’s length. Where does the dot vanish, and why?")
add(54, "A surgeon can stop your heart, cool your brain and restart both, and ask you afterward what you remember. Science can now tell from brain activity alone which unresponsive patients are aware. It cannot yet say why awareness feels like something.",
 "How do we know that anesthetics remove consciousness and not just memory? Brain responses and behavior with an isolated arm that is not anesthetized show who is aware during an operation.",
 "What kind of evidence would make you accept that a machine has experience?",
 "Describe the color red to someone who has never seen it. What can you not put into words?")
add(55, "An octopus unscrews a jar to reach the crab inside. A crow bends a wire into a hook. A dolphin recognizes itself in a mirror. Intelligence has arisen more than once, in brains laid out in very different ways, and each case is a clue to what intelligence needs.",
 "How do we know that an animal is thinking and not simply conditioned? Careful controls, new problems the animal has not met, and repeated results in different laboratories.",
 "What result would make you doubt that an animal passes a self-recognition test for the reason we think?",
 "Design an experiment to show whether a bird understands that food it has seen hidden is still there when out of sight.")
add(56, "No one has ever drilled deeper than about twelve kilometers, and yet we know that the Earth’s core is liquid iron 2,900 kilometers down. The evidence comes from earthquakes, which ring the whole planet like a bell.",
 "How do we know the outer core is liquid? Shear waves from earthquakes cannot pass through liquid, and they are missing on the far side of the Earth.",
 "A shear wave detected passing through the outer core would overturn the model. Why has none been seen?",
 "Cut out South America and Africa from a printed map of the continents. How well do they fit, and what does the fit not explain?")
add(57, "The physics of the greenhouse effect is older than the telephone. Eunice Foote noticed in 1856 that jars of carbon dioxide warmed in sunlight more than jars of air, and the measurements of the past century have confirmed what she saw.",
 "How do we know that the added carbon dioxide comes from fossil fuels? The carbon isotopes in the air have the signature of ancient plants, and the oxygen is falling by the amount burning requires.",
 "What observation would show that the Sun, and not carbon dioxide, is driving recent warming? Satellite measurements show that the Sun’s output has been flat or slightly falling since the 1980s.",
 "Look up the carbon-dioxide record from Mauna Loa and find the yearly sawtooth in it. What causes it?")
add(58, "Take wolves out of a park and the willows along the river may change. Take a bee out of an orchard and the fruit may fail. Ecology is the science of long chains of consequence, in which every link is a living thing.",
 "How do we know that a species is a keystone? Experimental removal, such as Robert Paine’s removal of a starfish from tide pools, showed how many other species depended on it.",
 "What would a food web look like if the keystone idea were wrong?",
 "Draw a food web for a pond or a garden with at least ten species. Remove one and trace what changes.")
add(59, "About two million species have been named, and the true total may be several times that. Most are insects, microbes and fungi, and most are unnamed, which makes extinction a hard thing to count.",
 "How do we know the rate of extinction? Comparisons with the fossil record, careful lists of well-studied groups and models of habitat loss are used, each with wide uncertainty.",
 "What evidence would tell you that a species long unseen is extinct and not just hiding?",
 "Spend ten minutes counting kinds of life in a square meter of lawn or a hedge. How many can you tell apart?")
add(60, "The ocean covers seventy percent of the planet and has absorbed about ninety percent of the extra heat we have trapped. Less of its floor has been mapped at high resolution than the surface of Mars.",
 "How do we know that the ocean is acidifying? Long series of measurements at Hawaii and Bermuda show its pH falling, by about 0.1 over the past century.",
 "What would show that corals could adapt to warmer water quickly enough to survive?",
 "Add a little purple cabbage juice to water and blow gently through a straw into it. The color changes because dissolved carbon dioxide has acidified it. What is the same process doing to the sea?")
add(61, "The sunlight that reaches Earth in an hour is more than humanity uses in a year. The difficulty is not supply but harvest, storage and wires.",
 "How do we know that the costs have fallen? Prices of panels, turbines and batteries have been published for decades, and the declines are among the best-documented learning curves.",
 "What would persuade you that a grid can run on mostly wind and solar? Look at real grids that have reached a high share and what they needed.",
 "Find your monthly electricity use on a bill. A good panel yields about 0.8 kilowatt-hours per square meter per day. How much panel area would supply it?")
add(62, "Sustainability is arithmetic before it is politics: how fast is a stock drawn down, and how fast does it refill? A bank account, an aquifer and a fishery all obey the same rule, and in many cases the numbers are known.",
 "How do we know that an aquifer is being overdrawn? Wells show falling water levels, and satellites that weigh the water from orbit show the change over regions.",
 "What would count as evidence that a resource is truly renewable at the rate we use it?",
 "Estimate your household’s yearly use of water, electricity or fuel and compare it with the average for your country.")
add(63, "In 1948 Claude Shannon showed that every message has a size, measured in bits, that no cleverness can beat, and that noise can be defeated by adding the right redundancy. Every phone call, photograph and spacecraft signal obeys the limits he proved.",
 "How do we know that the limits are reachable? Codes invented decades after Shannon’s theorem now come within a fraction of a decibel of them.",
 "A compression scheme that claimed to shrink every possible file would break a counting argument. Why?",
 "Play twenty questions with a friend. How many yes-or-no questions does it take to single out one of a million items? (Answer: about 20.)")
add(64, "Each time you see a padlock in a browser, two machines that have never met have agreed a secret in public, using mathematics that was a government secret until the 1970s. The security is a bet that certain problems are hard, and the bet is being tested by quantum computers.",
 "How do we know a code is secure? Experts attack it in public for years, and for some systems proofs reduce its security to a hard problem.",
 "What announcement would tell you that the mathematics behind the padlock was no longer safe?",
 "Write a short message with a Caesar shift of three. Then try to break a friend’s shifted message by counting letters, and say why the attack works better on longer text.")
add(65, "A program trained only to predict the next word can write code, explain physics and pass professional exams, and it can state a falsehood in the same confident tone. Understanding what such systems are and are not is now part of scientific literacy.",
 "How do we know whether a model understands and does not merely repeat? Tests on problems it could not have seen, probing of its internal states and careful comparison with human reasoning all help, and none is conclusive.",
 "What result would persuade you that a system has reasoned and not recalled?",
 "Ask a chat system something whose answer you know very well, and then something you can check. Compare how sure it sounds with how right it is.")
add(66, "A factory arm repeats a weld ten thousand times without error, but a home robot that folds laundry reliably is still a research goal. The gap between the factory and the kitchen is the whole problem of robotics.",
 "How do we know a robot demonstration is ready to be a product? Look at the success rate over thousands of trials, the cost per task and whether it works away from the lab.",
 "What failure rate per step would make a ten-step household task practical?",
 "List ten steps in making a cup of tea. If each step has a 99 percent chance of success, what is the chance that all ten succeed? (Answer: about 90 percent.)")
add(67, "Insulin for people with diabetes was once extracted from the pancreases of cattle and pigs. Since 1982 it has been made by bacteria carrying the human gene, and most of modern medicine’s newest drugs now come from living cells in steel tanks.",
 "How do we know a biological medicine is safe and works? Trials test it in people, and manufacturing is controlled so that every batch is the same.",
 "What would you want to see before accepting a gene drive release in the wild?",
 "Find the active ingredient of a medicine in your home. Does it come from a plant, a microbe or a chemical factory?")
add(68, "Voyager 1, launched in 1977, is now more than 24 billion kilometers from Earth and still sends data home using a transmitter weaker than a refrigerator lamp.",
 "How do we know what is on other worlds? Spacecraft carry instruments that measure what no telescope can, and returned samples can be tested in laboratories.",
 "What single measurement from a spacecraft would most change our view of whether Mars once had life?",
 "Find out when the International Space Station will next pass over your town, and watch it cross the sky.")
add(69, "Aristotle held that heavy things fall faster, and for nearly two thousand years almost no one tried dropping two weights. Science is not a body of facts but a habit that took a long time to form: ask what would count as evidence, and then look.",
 "How do we know when one theory should replace another? When it predicts new facts, explains the old ones and survives tests the old theory fails.",
 "Pick a theory in this book. What evidence would make you abandon it?",
 "Choose a belief once held by experts and since overturned. What evidence overturned it, and how long did that take?")
add(70, "Planck, Born, von Laue and Tegmark share a first name and an interest in what mathematics says about reality. Between them they stand for the quantum, probability, the first view of atoms in crystals and the idea that the universe is mathematics all the way down.",
 "How do we know the value of Planck’s constant? It can be found from the black-body curve, the photoelectric effect and the quantum Hall effect, which agree.",
 "What kind of result would show that the mathematical-universe idea is wrong, or that it cannot be tested?",
 "Find the energy of a green photon, with frequency 5.5 × 10¹⁴ hertz, from E = hf. (Answer: about 3.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ joules, or 2.3 electron-volts.)")
add(71, "A coin lands heads five times in a row, and nobody is surprised. It lands heads fifty times and everyone is. The difference between the two reactions is statistics, which is how science decides what to believe.",
 "How do we know a result will replicate? We cannot be sure, but pre-registered plans, large samples and independent repetition raise the odds.",
 "What would persuade you that a study’s finding had come from chance?",
 "Flip a coin 100 times. If you got 60 heads, how surprising is that? Look up or compute the probability of 60 or more. (Answer: about 3 percent.)")
add(72, "Eugene Wigner called the usefulness of mathematics in physics “unreasonable.” The same mathematics that lets us count apples also shows that some true statements about numbers can never be proved.",
 "How do we know that a proof is correct? Experts check it, and for some proofs a computer verifies every step against the rules.",
 "What would an inconsistency in arithmetic mean, and why do mathematicians think we would find out?",
 "Draw squares to show that the sum of the first n odd numbers is n squared.")
add(73, "The most reliable theories in physics have each been replaced by something better, and each is still used. Learning to hold a result firmly and loosely at the same time is the central skill of living with science.",
 "How do we know whom to trust? Look at track record, whether claims can be checked and whether an expert’s incentives line up with the truth.",
 "What would you want to see before changing your mind about something you believe strongly?",
 "Take a news headline announcing a scientific breakthrough and find the original paper’s abstract. What did the paper actually claim?")
add(74, "Every era’s open problems read, a century later, like a list of what was about to be solved or about to be misunderstood. The present list is long because the answers of the last hundred years raised sharper questions.",
 "How do we know that a problem is open and not merely unfamiliar? It appears in the current literature and has no accepted answer.",
 "Pick one problem on the list. What observation would count against your favorite answer?",
 "Choose an open question from this chapter and write down an experiment or observation that would help settle it.")
add(75, "Forecasts of the future fail on dates and succeed on trends. This chapter keeps to what present measurements make likely, separates it from hope, and marks where the choices are ours.",
 "How do we know which forecasts to trust? Those tied to measured rates and to physical limits do better than those that depend on a single invention arriving on time.",
 "What development would make you revise the forecasts in this chapter most?",
 "Find a forecast made in 1990 about a technology and score how it did.")
add(76, "Every guide to science dates itself within a decade. The final chapter lists what a successor would have to add, replace or retire, and so tells you which parts of this one to hold most loosely.",
 "How do we know which statements will last? Those that rest on repeated measurements and on several lines of evidence tend to.",
 "Which statement in this book would you most like to see tested again?",
 "Mark three statements in this book that you find least convincing and look each one up in a primary source.")
